const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_ii4_72",
  "series": "Band II 4 (1701 - 1707)",
  "seq": 72,
  "text": "<p> [[240.8]] <b>72. DAMARIS LADY MASHAM AN LEIBNIZ</p>\n<p> [[240.9]] Oates, 3. (14.) Juni 1704. [67.76.] </b></p>\n<p> [[240.25]] S^#'r Oates 3 June 1704. </p>\n<p> [[240.26]] Your great Civilitie Obliges me not the less for haveing presum'd from your Goodness\n[[240.27]] upon more than I had any Right to Pretend to when I requested you would give your Self the\n[[240.28]] Pains of informing me farther concerning your Hypothesis. </p>\n<p> [[240.29]] To a Mind Possess'd in any measure with a Due Admiration of the works of God Nothing\n[[240.30]] is more Gratefull than by farther discoveries therein of his Divine Perfections, to be sensibly\n[[240.31]] engag'd to Adore that Being which Reason pronounces ought to be the supreme Object of our\n[[240.32]] Affections. </p>\n<p> [[241.1]] An Hypothesis thought by your self and others conduceing to such an End as this, could\n[[241.2]] not but excite my enquirie; and it did so the more because that in reading what you have\n[[241.3]] publish'd, it seem'd to my self also you had views there in which gave a very Becomeing Idea\n[[241.4]] of the Wisedome of God in his Works. The letter you have favour'd me with confirms me in\n[[241.5]] this Thought: but that I may be sure of haveing a certain and cleare knōledge of your\n[[241.6]] Hypothesis permit me to tell you what I conceive it to be; in hopes you will rectifie my\n[[241.7]] Mistakes if I am in any; which is what may easilie happen to one so little conversant as I am in\n[[241.8]] such Speculations. </p>\n<p> [[241.9]] You take for granted that <b>there is a Simple Being in us indu'd with Action and\n[[241.10]] Perception.</b> The same, you say, <b>differing onely in the manner of Perception, is in\n[[241.11]] matter everie where</b>. That that <b>Simple Being</b> in us, which is call'd <b>Soul</b> is distinguish'd\n[[241.12]] from that of Beasts (and yet more from that of other Bodys about us) by the Power of\n[[241.13]] <b>Abstraction</b> and frameing thereby Universal Ideas. All these <b>Simple Beings</b> you think\n[[241.14]] have; Always will have; and ever since they existed have had, <b>Organick Bodys, proportion'd\n[[241.15]] to theire Perception.</b> So that not onely after Death the <b>Soul</b> dos remain, but even\n[[241.16]] the <b>Animal</b> also. Generation, and Death, being but a displaying or Concealment of these\n[[241.17]] Beings <k>to</k>, or <k>from</k> our view. The same Principle of Uniformitie in the Works of Nature which\n[[241.18]] has led you to beleeve this has, you say, led you also to your Systeme of the <b>Harmonie\n[[241.19]] Preestablish'd betweene Substances</b>. the which I thus understand. </p>\n<p> [[241.20]] Any Action of the soul upon Matter, or of Matter upon the Soul is Inconceivable: These\n[[241.21]] two have theire Laws distinct. Bodies follow the Laws of Mechanisme, and have a tendencie to\n[[241.22]] change <b><i>suivant les Forces Mouvantes</i>. Souls produce in themselves Internal\n[[241.23]] Actions</b> and have a tendencie to change <b>according to the Perception that they have of\n[[241.24]] Good or Ill.</b> Now <b>Soul</b> and <b>Body</b>, following each theire Proper Laws, and neither of them\n[[241.25]] acting thereby upon, or Affecting the other, such Effects are yet produc'd from a <b>Harmonie\n[[241.26]] Preetablish'd betwixt these Substances,</b> as if there was a real communication betweene\n[[241.27]] them. So that the Body acting constantly by its owne Laws of Mechanisme without receiving\n[[241.28]] any Variation or change therein from any Action of the Soul dos yet always correspond to the\n[[241.29]] Passions and Perceptions which the Soul hath. And the Soul, in like Manner, thō not operated\n[[241.30]] upon by the Motions of Matter, has yet at the same time that the Body Acts according to its\n[[241.31]] Laws of Mechanisme, certain Perceptions or Modifications which fail not to answer thereunto. </p>\n<p> [[241.32]] This is what I conceive you to say; in which (to tell you Thoughts so insignificant as mine)\n[[241.33]] I see nothing, Peculiar, which seems not possible. I find a Uniformitie in it which pleases me:\n[[241.34]] and the Advantages propos'd from this Hypothesis are very desirable. But it appears not yet to\n[[241.35]] me that This is more than an Hypothesis; for as Gods ways are not limited by our Conceptions;\n[[241.36]] the unintelligibleness or inconceivablness by us of any way but one, dos not, methinks, much\n[[242.1]] induce a Beleefe of that, being the way which God has chosen to make use of. Yet such an\n[[242.2]] inference as this from our Ignorance, I remember P. Malbranche (or some other assertor of his\n[[242.3]] Hypothesis) would make in behalf of <k>Occasional Causes:</k> to which Hypothesis, amongst\n[[242.4]] other exceptions, I think there is one, which I cannot, without your help, see, but that yours is\n[[242.5]] alike liable to and that is, from the Organization of the Body: wherin all that Nice Curiositie\n[[242.6]] that is discoverable seeming Useless: becomes Superfluous and lost labour. To this difficultie\n[[242.7]] likewise let me add that I conceive not why <b>Organisme</b> should be, or can be thought, as you\n[[242.8]] say is, <b>Essential to Matter.</b> </p>\n<p> [[242.9]] But these enquiries, or others, that might it may be on further thoughts occur to me, are\n[[242.10]] less pertinent for me to make, than such a one as is more fundamental, althō it dos not\n[[242.11]] peculiarly respect your Hypothesis. </p>\n<p> [[242.12]] <b>Forms</b>, explain'd by you to signifie <b>Simple Beings</b>, you elsewhere call <b><i>Atomes des\n[[242.13]] Substances</b></i>, and <b><i>Forces Primitives,</b></i> the Nature whereof you, in another place, say, you\n[[242.14]] find <b>to Consist in Force.</b> </p>\n<p> [[242.15]] <b>Force</b>, I presume cannot be the essence of any Substance, but is the attribute of what you\n[[242.16]] call a <b>Form</b>, <b>Soul</b> or <b><i>Atome de Substance,</b></i> of the essence whereof I find no positive Idea,\n[[242.17]] and your negation of theire haveing any Dimensions, makes theire Existence, I confess,\n[[242.18]] inconceivable to me; as not being able to conceive an existence of that which <b>is No Where</b>. If\n[[242.19]] the Localitie of these Substances were accounted for by theire being as you [say] they are\n[[242.20]] always in Organiz'd Bodies, then they are somewhere: But if these <b><i>Atomes des Substances</b></i>\n[[242.21]] are somewhere then they must have some extension, which you deny of them: who, I think, also\n[[242.22]] place the Union of the Soul with its respective Body in nothing else but that correspondence or\n[[242.23]] conformitie whereby, in virtue of a <b>Preestablish'd Harmonie, Souls</b> and <b>Bodys</b>, Acting\n[[242.24]] apart, each by theire owne laws, the same effects are produc'd as if theire was a real Communication\n[[242.25]] betwixt them. Thō whether, or no, I perfectly comprehend your meaning in this part\n[[242.26]] I am in doubt. </p>\n<p> [[242.27]] What I have here said I think enough for me to venture to trouble you with at once: and it\n[[242.28]] will perhaps be more than enough to show you that you have judg'd by much too favorably of\n[[242.29]] my Apprehension. For I remember my Father as well as other Assertors of Unextended\n[[242.30]] Substance to have said <b>That it is an imposition of Imagination upon theire Reason in\n[[242.31]] Those who cannot be convinc'd of the realitie of substances unextended.</b> So great\n[[242.32]] Authoritie as His was to me could not hinder me, if this be so, from being always under such an\n[[243.1]] influence of Imagination; which is what would not willingly be in any case. But wherever I\n[[243.2]] have no Idea of a thing; or Demonstration of the Truth of any Proposition[,] the Truth of which\n[[243.3]] is inconceivable by me, I cannot, and conclude that I ought not to assent to what is asserted of\n[[243.4]] either: since should I once do this I know not where I should stop; what should be the\n[[243.5]] Boundaries of Assent. Or why I might not Beleeve alike one thing as well as another. </p>\n<p> [[243.6]] M^#'r Locke Presents you his humble service, and desires me to tell you He takes himself to\n[[243.7]] be mightilie oblig'd to you for your great Civilitie express'd to him; in which he finds you a\n[[243.8]] master as well as in Philosophy and every thing else. His want of Health he says now, and the\n[[243.9]] little remains he counts he has of Life, has put an end, to his enquiries into Philosophical\n[[243.10]] Speculations: thō if he were still in the heat of that Pursuit he could not be so Ignorant of You or\n[[243.11]] Himself as to take upon him to be the Judge of what you have well consider'd: much less to be\n[[243.12]] the Instructor of a man of your knowne extraordinarie Parts and merit. He takes it for a great\n[[243.13]] Honour done him, that you have Condescended to read and consider, as you say, his <k>Essay\n[[243.14]] upon the understanding</k> and that you think it worth the while by your Larger Views to\n[[243.15]] remove some difficulties, and supply some defects that are therein. This if he had any other end\n[[243.16]] in publishing that Treatise but some small service to Truth and Knōledge, would flatter his\n[[243.17]] vanitie. That it would be Preserv'd to Posteritie by the touches of so Great a Master: by whose\n[[243.18]] Hand it would be redeem'd from some of its own Imperfections. </p>\n<p> [[243.19]] All M^#'r Lockes Freinds have at present the greife to apprehend that They shall enjoy the\n[[243.20]] Happyness of his Freindship but a little time: the infirmitie of Ill Lungs dayly increaseing upon\n[[243.21]] him, in an Age that is considerable. </p>\n<p> [[243.22]] I am much Pleas'd S^#'r to have made you a Present, that is both acceptable to you, and that\n[[243.23]] can possibly contribute any thing to your inriching the world by the effects of your Meditations.\n[[243.24]] If you shall think me Worthy of your Instruction; or at any time of any Communication of your\n[[243.25]] Thoughts I shall always (as I ought to do) look upon it as a condescention in you which will\n[[243.26]] oblige me to be with the greatest acknowledgment as well as Esteeme </p>\n<p> [[243.27]] S^#'r Your Most Humble Servant Da Masham. </p>\n",
  "number": "72.",
  "title": "DAMARIS LADY MASHAM AN LEIBNIZ",
  "date": "Oates, 3. (14.) Juni 1704. [67.76.]"
};
